"Internal Error ()\nciteturn15view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pure.mpg.de/rest/items/item_1602419_3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn15view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pure.mpg.de/rest/items/item_1580900_1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDnaK Functions as a Central Hub in the E.&nbsp;coli Chaperone Network (https://pure.mpg.de/rest/items/item_1602419_3/component/file_1836221/content)\nciteturn15view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view0\",\"lineno\":710}); Total lines: 1383\nL626@P6: Increased in\nL627@P6: ΔT/K proteome\nL628@P6: (27)\nL629@P6: Decreased in\nL630@P6: ΔT/K proteome\nL631@P6: (40)\nL632@P6: 0 50 100 150 200 250 300 350 400\nL633@P6: 0.0\nL634@P6: 0.5\nL635@P6: 1.0\nL636@P6: 1.5\nL637@P6: 2.0\nL638@P6: 2.5\nL639@P6: 3.0\nL640@P6: 3.5\nL641@P6: OD600nm\nL642@P6: secretory\nL643@P6: proteins\nL644@P6: chaperones;\nL645@P6: proteases\nL646@P6: ribosomal &\nL647@P6: other small\nL648@P6: proteins\nL649@P6: His\nL650@P6: His\nL651@P6: His His\nL652@P6: His\nL653@P6: His\nL654@P6: His\nL655@P6: DnaK-interactors\nL656@P6: Increased on DnaK in ΔT/K\nL657@P6: Decreased on DnaK in ΔT/K\nL658@P6: His\nL659@P6: His\nL660@P6: >100\nL661@P6: <10\nL672@P6: (A) Overlap of DnaK interactors in KHis (672 proteins) and DT/KHis cells (998 proteins).\nL673@P6: (B and C) Change in the distribution of molecular weight (B) and isoelectric point (C) of the DnaK interactome in DT/KHis cells compared to all DnaK interactors\nL674@P6: in KHis cells.\nL675@P6: (D) Minimal overlap of the sets of proteins significantly decreased or increased in abundance in the proteome of DT/KHis cells with the set of proteins that\nL676@P6: increased on DnaK in DT/KHis cells.\nL677@P6: (E) Outer membrane destabilization in DT/KHis cells. Growth curves at 37\u0003C of KHis and DT/KHis cells in the presence and absence of 0.1% deoxycholate or\nL678@P6: 50 mg/ml vancomycin. Arrow indicates the time of addition of deoxycholate or vancomycin after dilution of an overnight culture in fresh M63 medium at an OD600nm\nL679@P6: of 0.025. Error bars represent SD of three independent measurements.\nL680@P6-7: Cell Reports 1, 251–264, March 29, 2012 ª2012 The Authors 257proteins have physico-chemical properties similar to the\nL681@P7: DnaK interactors in WT cells (data not shown). To detect quanti\u0002tative changes in the DnaK interactome upon TF deletion, we\nL682@P7: performed a comparative analysis of H-labeled DT/KHis and\nL683@P7: M-labeled KHis cells. Seventy-one proteins interacted to a signif\u0002icantly greater extent (1.4- to 3.3-fold) and 74 proteins to a lesser\nL684@P7: extent with DnaK in DT/KHis cells (Tables S6A and S6B). The\nL685@P7: proteins accumulating on DnaK are typically small in size\nL686@P7: (<20 kDa) (Figure 4B) and include 17 highly basic ribosomal\nL687@P7: proteins as well as ten basic nonribosomal proteins (pI R 9)\nL688@P7: (Figure 4C), such as several secretory proteins with functions\nL689@P7: in cell envelope and outer membrane biogenesis (Skp, AmpH,\nL690@P7: YcgK, SlyB) (Table S6A). The proteins that interacted less exten\u0002sively with DnaK in DT/KHis cells are larger in size (Figure 4B) and\nL691@P7: overlap with the large newly synthesized DnaK interactors\nL692@P7: described above (Figure 3B). Apparently, they bind to DnaK\nL693@P7: less efficiently in the absence of TF (Figure 4B).\nL694@P7: We also performed a proteomic analysis of total cell lysate of\nL695@P7: DT/KHis cells as compared to KHis cells. Only 40 of 1,490 quanti\u0002fied proteins were reproducibly reduced in abundance by 25%\u0004\nL696@P7: 85% (p < 0.05) when TF was deleted (Table S6C), although their\nL697@P7: rates of synthesis were unchanged (Figure S4); 27 proteins were\nL698@P7: increased in abundance (Figure 4D and Table S6D). Both these\nL699@P7: groups of proteins show minimal overlap with the proteins that\nL700@P7: accumulated on DnaK upon TF deletion (Figure 4D). Strikingly,\nL701@P7: most of the proteins that decreased in DT/KHis cells carry pre\u0002dicted signal sequences (Table S6C). These proteins include\nL702@P7: numerous outer membrane b-barrel proteins, suggesting that\nL703@P7: TF has a specific role in outer membrane protein biogenesis.\nL704@P7: Indeed, DT/KHis cells proved to be sensitive to treatment with\nL705@P7: detergents like deoxycholate and the antibiotic vancomycin,\nL706@P7: which is indicative of a weakening of the outer membrane\nL707@P7: (Figure 4E) (Nichols et al., 2011). The proteins that increased in\nL708@P7: DT/KHis cells include cytosolic chaperones and proteases, as\nL709@P7: well as the ATPase SecA required for membrane translocation\nL710@P7: of outer membrane proteins (Table S6D). However, these\nL711@P7: proteins are only moderately upregulated (\u00011.5-fold), suggest\u0002ing that loss of TF function at 37\u0003C causes only limited proteome\nL712@P7: stress.\nL713@P7: These results indicate a functional redundancy of TF and DnaK\nL714@P7: in the folding/assembly of ribosomal and other small, basic\nL715@P7: proteins. In addition, TF has a specific role in the biogenesis\nL716@P7: of outer membrane b-barrel proteins. This function cannot be\nL717@P7: performed by DnaK and is only partially replaced by other chap\u0002erones, resulting in outer membrane destabilization.\nL718@P7: Interplay of DnaK and GroEL/ES\nL719@P7: GroEL and its cofactor GroES are upregulated in both the DKJ\nL720@P7: and DT/KHis cells (Tables S2 and S6D), suggesting that these\nL721@P7: chaperone systems form a functional network. Strikingly, 119\nL722@P7: of the identified DnaK interactors are known GroEL substrates\nL723@P7: (Fujiwara et al., 2010; Kerner et al., 2005), and together these\nL724@P7: proteins amount to \u000130% of all DnaK interactors by mass.\nL725@P7: The number of GroEL substrates on DnaK increases to 152 in\nL726@P7: DT/KHis cells (Figure 5A, Table S1, and Proteome Commons\nL727@P7: Tranche repository). Forty-two of these DnaK interactors are\nL728@P7: obligate GroEL-dependent (class III) and thus must be delivered\nL729@P7: to GroEL for folding, whereas 80 belong to class II and 30 to class\nL730@P7: I (Figure 5B). Class II substrates are highly chaperone dependent\nL731@P7: but can utilize either GroEL/ES or DnaK/DnaJ for folding in vitro,\nL732@P7: whereas class I proteins have a lower chaperone dependence\nL733@P7: (Kerner et al., 2005). About 90 of the previously identified GroEL\nL734@P7: substrates were not detected on DnaK. Many of these proteins\nL735@P7: are of low abundance and thus may have very low steady-state\nL736@P7: levels on DnaK.\nL737@P7: To investigate how the depletion of GroEL/ES affects the spec\u0002trum of DnaK interactors, KHis cells carrying the groELS operon\nL738@P7: under arabinose control were shifted from arabinose (LS+/KHis)\nL739@P7: to glucose for 3.5 hr (LS\u0004/KHis) at 37\u0003C, which resulted in \u000197%\nL740@P7: depletion of GroEL/ES (Kerner et al., 2005; McLennan and\nL741@P7: Masters, 1998). Note that the cells grow normally during the first\nL742@P7: 5 hr of GroEL/ES depletion (data not shown). Ninety-two proteins\nL743@P7: increased on DnaK (2- to 60-fold) upon GroEL depletion and 54\nL744@P7: proteins decreased (Tables S7A and S7B). The former include\nL745@P7: 38 GroEL substrates (19 class III proteins) (Figure 5B). They are\nL746@P7: enriched in domains with SCOP fold c.1 (TIM-barrel) (Figure S5A),\nL747@P7: which is prominently represented among obligate GroEL sub\u0002strates (Fujiwara et al., 2010; Kerner et al., 2005) (Table S7A).\nL748@P7: The proteins depleted from DnaK include 11 GroEL substrates,\nL749@P7: mostly of class II (Table S7B), suggesting that they are partially dis\u0002placed from DnaK by class III substrates that are unable to fold.\nL750@P7: We also analyzed the consequences of GroEL depletion at the\nL751@P7: proteome level. Depletion of GroEL resulted in a \u000135%\u000495%\nL752@P7: decrease in abundance of 114 proteins and a R2-fold increase\nL753@P7: of 95 proteins (Tables S7C and S7D). The former include 44\nL754@P7: GroEL substrates (24 class III proteins) (Figure 5B and Table\nL755@P7: S7C) that are apparently degraded (Figure S5B). Strikingly, 18\nL756@P7: of these GroEL substrates nevertheless accumulated on DnaK\nL757@P7: (Figure 5C), suggesting that they are stabilized by DnaK for trans\u0002fer to the degradation machinery. The proteins that are upregu\u0002lated upon GroEL depletion include chaperones and proteases\nL758@P7: (upregulated \u00012-fold) as well as 19 GroEL substrates (Table\nL759@P7: S7C). In addition to degradation, loss of GroEL function at 37\u0003C\nL760@P7: also resulted in substantial aggregation of many obligate GroEL\nL761@P7: substrates (Figure 5D) (Chapman et al., 2006; Kerner et al., 2005).\nL762@P7: In summary, GroEL substrates interact extensively with DnaK.\nL763@P7: Upon depletion of GroEL, obligate GroEL substrates accumulate\nL764@P7: further on DnaK and are either transferred to the degradation\nL765@P7: machinery or eventually aggregate.\nL766@P7: Proteostasis Collapse upon Combined Deletion\nL767@P7: of DnaK and TF\nL768@P7: Prevention of protein aggregation is considered fundamental\nL769@P7: to proteostasis maintenance (Hartl et al., 2011). To define the\nL770@P7: relative contribution of the different chaperone systems to\nL771@P7: aggregation prevention, we performed a comparative analysis\nL772@P7: of the aggregated proteomes upon individual and combined\nL773@P7: chaperone deletion at 30\u0003C, where cells lacking DnaK/DnaJ\nL774@P7: and TF (DKJT) still grow, albeit slowly (Figure 1B and Figure S1A).\nL775@P7: As described above, 474 proteins increased significantly in the\nL776@P7: insoluble fraction of DKJ cells, compared to only 15 proteins in\nL777@P7: DT/KHis cells and 33 proteins in GroEL/ES depleted (LS\u0004/KHis)\nL778@P7: cells (Figure 6A and Table S8), indicating a major role of the\nL779@P7: DnaK system in aggregation prevention. Upon combined dele\u0002tion of DnaK/DnaJ and TF, 1,087 proteins aggregated, including\nL780@P7: 403 DnaK interactors identified in this study (Figure 6A and\nL781@P7-8: 258 Cell Reports 1, 251–264, March 29, 2012 ª2012 The AuthorsTable S8). The aggregated proteins also included 149 of a total of\nL782@P8: 196 proteins shown previously to aggregate in cells lacking\nL783@P8: DnaK/DnaJ and TF (Deuerling et al., 2003; Martinez-Hackert\nL784@P8: and Hendrickson, 2009). Moreover, whereas in DKJ cells only\nL785@P8: 65 proteins aggregated substantially (>5% depletion from the\nL786@P8: soluble fraction), the number of substantially aggregated pro\u0002teins increased 4-fold upon additional loss of TF (Figure 6A\nL787@P8: and Table S8). Strikingly, the size distribution of these proteins\nL788@P8: showed a strong shift to large proteins >50 KDa, frequently con\u0002taining multiple domains (Figure 6B and Table S8). Many of these\nL789@P8: proteins (\u000150%) are identified DnaK substrates of average or\nL790@P8: above average abundance (Table S1) and represent a subset\nL791@P8: of the large proteins that interact with DnaK on synthesis (Fig\u0002ure 3B). Apparently, these proteins are chaperone-dependent\nL792@P8: but can utilize either the DnaK system or TF for efficient folding.\nL793@P8: Remarkably, \u000170% of the previously identified GroEL sub\u0002strates (167 proteins) (Kerner et al., 2005) were also recovered\nL794@P8: in the aggregate fraction of DKJT cells (Figure 6A), many of which\nL795@P8: aggregated substantially (Table S8). This effect was observed\nL796@P8: despite a \u000110-fold upregulation of GroEL/ES (data not shown).\nL797@P8: Aggregation of GroEL substrates was essentially undetectable\nL798@P8: in DKJ or DT/KHis cells at 30\u0003C (Table S8). Thus, substrate\nL799@P8: delivery to GroEL critically depends on the upstream chaperones\nL800@P8: but can be performed by either DnaK or TF.\nL801@P8: A large number of ribosomal proteins also accumulated in the\nL802@P8: insoluble fraction of DKJT cells, almost all of which were identi\u0002fied as DnaK interactors (Table S8). Deletion of DnaK/DnaJ alone\nL803@P8: resulted in the aggregation of only three ribosomal proteins,\nL804@P8: whereas none of these proteins aggregated in the DT/KHis cells.\nL805@P8: Although aggregation did not cause a significant depletion of\nL806@P8: ribosomal proteins from the soluble fraction, these results sug\u0002gested that DKJT cells have a defect in ribosomal biogenesis.\nL807@P8: Indeed, numerous ribosomal proteins were reduced in abun\u0002dance by 10%\u000430% in total lysates of DKJT cells compared\nL808@P8: to KHis cells and the single chaperone deletions (Figure 6C). Inter\u0002estingly, two small ribosomal proteins (RpsQ S17 and RpsT S20)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDnaK Functions as a Central Hub in the E.&nbsp;coli Chaperone Network (https://pure.mpg.de/rest/items/item_1602419_3/component/file_1836221/content)\nciteturn15view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view0\",\"lineno\":1185}); Total lines: 1383\nL1133@P10: in the absence of TF defines in a quantitative\nL1134@P10: manner the functional redundancy between\nL1135@P10: these two chaperone systems described earlier\nL1136@P10: (Deuerling et al., 1999; Teter et al., 1999). Inter\u0002estingly, these substrates comprise mostly\nL1137@P10: ribosomal and other small (<20 kDa), positively\nL1138@P10: charged proteins (Figure 7B), which may nor\u0002mally interact predominantly with TF, but shift to DnaK when\nL1139@P10: TF is absent. Indeed, TF has a negative net charge (Ferbitz\nL1140@P10: et al., 2004; Martinez-Hackert and Hendrickson, 2009), which\nL1141@P10: may facilitate its interaction with positively charged nascent\nL1142@P10: polypeptides, and a role of TF in the folding/assembly of ribo\u0002somal proteins has been suggested (Martinez-Hackert and\nL1143@P10: Hendrickson, 2009).\nL1144@P10: In contrast, the DnaK system is unable to replace the role of TF\nL1145@P10: in the biogenesis of a set of secretory proteins, prominently\nL1146@P10: including b-barrel proteins of the outer membrane (Figure 7B).\nL1147@P10: These proteins undergo partial degradation in cells lacking TF,\nL1148@P10: suggesting a specific role of TF in translocation of outer mem\u0002brane preproteins across the inner membrane. Such a function\nL1149@P10: of TF would be consistent with the initial identification of TF as\nL1150@P10: a chaperone of proOmpA translocation in vitro (Crooke et al.,\nL1151@P10: 1988) and with the finding that TF modulates the kinetics of\nL1152@P10-11: Cell Reports 1, 251–264, March 29, 2012 ª2012 The Authors 261protein export (Lee and Bernstein, 2002; Ullers et al., 2007). It is\nL1153@P11: also of interest in this context that TF structurally resembles the\nL1154@P11: periplasmic chaperone for outer membrane proteins, SurA (Bitto\nL1155@P11: and McKay, 2002; Ferbitz et al., 2004).\nL1156@P11: An effective functional cooperation apparently exists between\nL1157@P11: TF and the DnaK system in the folding of a group of large multi\u0002domain proteins that aggregate substantially only in the absence\nL1158@P11: of both chaperones. These proteins may normally interact\nL1159@P11: sequentially with TF and DnaK during translation or with multiple\nL1160@P11: TF molecules in the absence of DnaK, as shown with large model\nL1161@P11: proteins in vitro (Agashe et al., 2004; Kaiser et al., 2006). Notably,\nL1162@P11: most of these proteins would be unable to interact productively\nL1163@P11: with GroEL, as the capacity of the GroEL/ES folding compart\u0002ment is limited to proteins up to \u000160 kDa (Kerner et al., 2005).\nL1164@P11: The DnaK interactome overlaps extensively with the set of\nL1165@P11: previously identified GroEL substrates (Figure 7B), most of which\nL1166@P11: are below 50 kDa in size (Kerner et al., 2005). GroEL substrates\nL1167@P11: amount to nearly 30% of the total mass of DnaK interactors, indi\u0002cating that protein transfer between DnaK and GroEL is a central\nL1168@P11: function of the chaperone network. We estimate that only a minor\nL1169@P11: fraction of GroEL substrates (\u000120%) are transferred directly from\nL1170@P11: TF to GroEL, circumventing DnaK (Figure 7B). Notably, upon\nL1171@P11: GroEL-depletion, obligate GroEL substrates accumulate further\nL1172@P11: on DnaK, reflecting an important role of DnaK as a buffer in stabi\u0002lizing these proteins until GroEL is available or in transferring\nL1173@P11: them to the proteolytic system.\nL1174@P11: Defining Proteostasis Collapse\nL1175@P11: Upon growth at 30\u0003C, the loss of individual chaperone\nL1176@P11: modules—DnaK/DnaJ, TF or GroEL/ES—is remarkably well\nL1177@P11: tolerated by E. coli cells in terms of preventing major protein\nL1178@P11: aggregation. Instead, degradation is the strongly preferred fate\nL1179@P11: of misfolded proteins under these conditions (Tables S2B,\nL1180@P11: S6C, and S7C). However, proteostasis collapse characterized\nL1181@P11: by extensive aggregation of relatively abundant proteins occurs\nL1182@P11: when TF is deleted in addition to DnaK/DnaJ. Apparently, the\nL1183@P11: chaperone capacity available for the folding and stabilization of\nL1184@P11: large proteins in particular becomes severely limiting and, as\nL1185@P11: a result, aggregation is favored relative to degradation. Further\u0002more, our data show that the loss of the upstream chaperones,\nL1186@P11: TF and DnaK/DnaJ, disrupts the normal protein flux to GroEL,\nL1187@P11: resulting in wide-spread aggregation of GroEL-substrates,\nL1188@P11: despite a \u000110-fold upregulation of GroEL under these condi\u0002tions. The failure of newly synthesized GroEL substrates to reach\nL1189@P11: the abundantly expressed chaperonin signifies the systematic\nL1190@P11: collapse of the chaperone network.\nL1191@P11: EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURES\nL1192@P11: Bacterial Strains\nL1193@P11: The E. coli strains used were based on MC4100 (WT) and are described in\nL1194@P11: Extended Experimental Procedures.\nL1195@P11: Isolation of DnaK-Interactor Complexes\nL1196@P11: SILAC labeling of cells was performed at 37\u0003C in M63 medium supplemented\nL1197@P11: with light (L), medium (M), or heavy (H) arginine and lysine isotopes (see\nL1198@P11: Extended Experimental Procedures). DnaK interactors were isolated from cells\nL1199@P11: growing exponentially (OD600nm \u00011). In pulse-SILAC experiments, L-labeled\nL1200@P11: cells were shifted to M-medium for 2.5 min; in pulse chase-SILAC experi\u0002ments, L-labeled cells were shifted to H-medium for 2 min and then chased\nL1201@P11: by addition of a 100-fold excess of L arginine and lysine for 2, 4, and 8 min.\nL1202@P11: Spheroplasts were prepared as described (Ewalt et al., 1997) and lysed in\nL1203@P11: hypo-osmotic buffer containing apyrase. Soluble cell lysate was prepared by\nL1204@P11: centrifugation (20,000 3 g, 30 min). Talon beads (Invitrogen) were used to\nL1205@P11: isolate the DnaK-His6 and its interactors. The eluates containing bound\nL1206@P11: proteins obtained from equal amounts of L-, M-, or H-labeled cells were mixed\nL1207@P11: (see Extended Experimental Procedures). Samples were prepared for LC-MS/\nL1208@P11: MS as described (Figure 1C) (Ong and Mann, 2006). The spectra were inter\u0002preted using MaxQuant version 1.0.13.13 (Cox and Mann, 2008) combined\nL1209@P11: with Mascot version 2.2 (Matrix Science, www.matrixscience.com).\nL1210@P11: The MaxQuant tables along with a full list of identified proteins and\nL1211@P11: quantitations are available at the Proteome Commons Tranche repository\nL1212@P11: (https://proteomecommons.org/) by inserting the following tranche code:\nL1213@P11: RKuFJcuu8iBnZHlBN4Nth0pz+HQgPNvV0zJvdnMN8wyAN8l7ifufEnmW6j2C\nL1214@P11: wn0msEakFC6eulEqlYv+7B+dFALonrgAAAAAAAAKDA== and passphrase:\nL1215@P11: vqXGUF93rGLCraqm1yIl.\nL1216@P11: The raw data is accessible using tranche codes and passphrase given in\nL1217@P11: Extended Experimental Procedures.\nL1218@P11: Fractionation of Total Cell Lysate\nL1219@P11: E. coli MC4100 dnaK-His6 (KHis) and chaperone mutant strains were grown to\nL1220@P11: OD600nm \u00011 at 30\u0003C or 37\u0003C, as indicated, in the respective SILAC medium.\nL1221@P11: Cells were collected, flash frozen, and lysed by sonication. Whole proteome\nL1222@P11: analyses on total lysates, soluble, and detergent insoluble fractions (Deuerling\nL1223@P11: et al., 2003) were performed by LC-MS/MS as described in Extended Exper\u0002imental Procedures.\nL1224@P11: Bioinformatic Analysis\nL1225@P11: Bioinformatics and statistical analyses of the physico-chemical properties of\nL1226@P11: protein sequences were performed as detailed in Extended Experimental\nL1227@P11: Procedures. Note that both DnaJ and GrpE, the cofactors of DnaK, are\nL1228@P11: excluded in all bioinformatic analyses.\nL1229@P11: SUPPLEMENTAL INFORMATION\nL1230@P11: Supplemental Information includes Extended Experimental Procedures,\nL1231@P11: five figures, and eight tables and can be found with this article online at\nL1232@P11: doi:10.1016/j.celrep.2011.12.007.\nL1233@P11: LICENSING INFORMATION\nL1234@P11: This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative\nL1235@P11: Commons Attribution-Noncommercial-No Derivative Works 3.0 Unported\nL1236@P11: License (CC-BY-NC-ND; http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/3.0/\nL1237@P11: legalcode).\nL1238@P11: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nL1239@P11: We thank R.B. Ko¨ rner and A. Ries for support with mass spectrometry and\nL1240@P11: S. Pinkert for his help with data management. Financial support from EU\nL1241@P11: Framework 7 Integrated Project PROSPECTS, the Deutsche Forschungsge\u0002meinschaft (SFB 594), and the Ko¨ rber Foundation is acknowledged. G.C.\nL1242@P11: was supported by an EMBO long-term fellowship.\nL1243@P11: Received: October 31, 2011\nL1244@P11: Revised: December 4, 2011\nL1245@P11: Accepted: December 23, 2011\nL1246@P11: Published online: March 8, 2012\nL1247@P11: REFERENCES\nL1248@P11: Agashe, V.R., Guha, S., Chang, H.C., Genevaux, P., Hayer-Hartl, M., Stemp,\nL1249@P11: M., Georgopoulos, C., Hartl, F.U., and Barral, J.M. (2004). Function of trigger\nL1250@P11: factor and DnaK in multidomain protein folding: increase in yield at the\nL1251@P11: expense of folding speed. Cell 117, 199–209.\nL1252@P11-12: 262 Cell Reports 1, 251–264, March 29, 2012 ª2012 The AuthorsBalch, W.E., Morimoto, R.I., Dillin, A., and Kelly, J.W. (2008). Adapting proteo\u0002stasis for disease intervention. Science 319, 916–919.\nL1253@P12: Bitto, E., and McKay, D.B. (2002). Crystallographic structure of SurA, a molec\u0002ular chaperone that facilitates folding of outer membrane porins. Structure 10,\nL1254@P12: 1489–1498.\nL1255@P12: Bukau, B., and Walker, G.C. (1989). Cellular defects caused by deletion of the\nL1256@P12: Escherichia coli dnaK gene indicate roles for heat shock protein in normal\nL1257@P12: metabolism. J. Bacteriol. 171, 2337–2346.\nL1258@P12: Chapman, E., Farr, G.W., Usaite, R., Furtak, K., Fenton, W.A., Chaudhuri, T.K.,\nL1259@P12: Hondorp, E.R., Matthews, R.G., Wolf, S.G., Yates, J.R., et al. (2006). Global\nL1260@P12: aggregation of newly translated proteins in an Escherichia coli strain deficient\nL1261@P12: of the chaperonin GroEL. Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA 103, 15800–15805.\nL1262@P12: Cox, J., and Mann, M. (2008). MaxQuant enables high peptide identification\nL1263@P12: rates, individuali